    "url": "https://assets.ctfassets.net/zqt8tllj2cy0/229Bgcvw5c0RwcUkhyghXG/d3722a14b4b1dd925316a8936200f5bd/MOES_1906368_Nutrition_Chart_2026_R4.pdf",
    "fetchedAt": "2026-08-07",
    "fetchedBy": "research agent, verified against the official source",
    "notes": "SOURCE: Every item macro below is transcribed verbatim from Moe's official 2026 nutrition chart PDF (MOES_1906368_Nutrition_Chart_2026_R4.pdf), which is linked directly from the moes.com menu page. moes.com blocks WebFetch with HTTP 403, but serves normally to a standard browser User-Agent; the PDF was retrieved that way. Preset published totals come from the product pages under https://www.moes.com/menu/ (baseCal/maxCal and per-product nutrition fields in the page payload).\n\nPORTION BASIS: The PDF states portion NAMES, not gram/ounce weights, so serving descriptions use Moe's own portion vocabulary. Moe's documents three protein tiers explicitly: Small = tacos/kids meal, Medium = burrito/bowls/salads/nachos/quesadillas/stacks, Double = double protein (protein-claim items). All three are included. Rice and beans differ materially between a burrito (ingredient portion) and a bowl (cup/burrito-bowl portion) — 150 vs 300 cal for seasoned rice — so both portions are listed as separate items rather than averaged.\n\nCROSS-CHECK: Every PDF row was validated by reconstructing calories from 4/4/9 (carbs/protein/fat). All rows agree within normal label-rounding, so the chart is internally consistent.\n\nDELIBERATELY NOT USED — the website's embedded per-item nutrition JSON. It is partially corrupted and contradicts the PDF: it lists the 170-calorie cookie with 74g carbs/41g sugar/16g fat, contains stray records such as \"calories\":\"9159\" and \"calories\":\"5906\", gives seasoned rice as 2g fat/66g carbs against the PDF's 6g fat/54g carbs, and attaches the 12\" flour tortilla's values (8g fat/51g carbs/8g protein) to the Homewrecker burrito product. The PDF is the trustworthy source and is the only source used for item-level macros.\n\nPRESETS — HOW PUBLISHED TOTALS WERE INTERPRETED (reverse-engineering disclosed): Moe's publishes a calorie RANGE per named meal, not a single total. Across every product on the site, maxCal = baseCal + 150 exactly, and 150 is precisely Ground Beef Medium, the highest-calorie protein. That establishes that baseCal is the meal WITHOUT protein and maxCal is the meal with the most caloric protein. This was confirmed independently: Homewrecker Bowl's published baseCal of 650 matches my protein-free component sum of 645 (and 650 + 150 = its published 800 max). Presets 1-4 therefore use baseCal as publishedCalories and contain NO protein component, which is exactly what Moe's publishes; users add a protein at build time.\n\nThe two Moe Protein items are the documented exception: double protein is intrinsic to the product, and the published \"up to 61g / 69g protein\" claims are unreachable without it (protein-free components give only 24g / 29g). Those presets therefore include White Meat Chicken Double, the highest-protein option, which reproduces the 61g claim almost exactly (60g computed).\n\nVERIFICATION DELTAS (component sum vs published, all consistently slightly LOW, which is the expected artifact of summing individually-rounded ingredient rows): Homewrecker 805 vs 810 (-0.6%); Homewrecker Bowl 645 vs 650 (-0.8%), protein 21 vs 23; Burrito Bowl 565 vs 600 (-5.8%), protein 20 vs 21; Custom Burrito 725 vs 760 (-4.6%), protein 25 vs 27; Moe Protein Bowl 850 vs 870 (-2.3%), protein 60 vs 61; Moe Protein Burrito 1010 vs 1040 (-2.9%), protein 65 vs 69. The Moe Protein Burrito has the weakest protein agreement (4g gap) and I could not account for it; its calorie agreement is fine. Homewrecker burrito uses publishedProtein -1 because the site's protein value for that product is the corrupted tortilla record described above.\n\nCONFIDENCE: High for the menu itself — every number is verbatim official and cross-checked. The preset published totals are official, but their component decomposition is inferred from Moe's own text descriptions of each meal, so treat preset composition as good-but-derived rather than published line items."
  },
